          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"bronte-jane-eyre-cddcbd01af3733b3\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"Jane’s utterance is principally a reflective avowal that her love for Rochester is now stronger, specifically because his changed circumstances allow her to care for and assist him. The surrounding acceptance of marriage gives it slight commitment-reinforcing force, but the sentence itself chiefly presents her emotional state as true. Nothing indicates that the words are a reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"There is limited T/P ambiguity because the avowal occurs during their renewed engagement and supports Jane’s undertaking to become Rochester’s wife, but its comparative and explanatory form remains predominantly truth-conditional.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Jane directly addresses Rochester during their conversation after accepting his marriage proposal.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Rochester fears that his blindness, disability, and dependence make marriage to him a sacrifice for Jane. She counters this by avowing that she loves him more now that she can be useful to him than when his pride confined him to the role of giver and protector. The statement reassures him and supports their prospective marriage, while also articulating Jane’s preference for a more reciprocal relationship.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I love you better now ... than I did in your state of proud independence”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit comparative presents Jane’s current love and its increased degree as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“when I can really be useful to you”\",\"supports\":\"Jane gives a considered reason for her stronger present love; this reflective explanation favors an avowal over reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately beforehand Jane accepts Rochester’s proposal—“Jane, will you marry me?” “Yes, sir.”—and says that becoming his wife will make her happy.\",\"supports\":\"The utterance occurs within an explicit marital undertaking, giving it some commitment-reinforcing pragmatic force, though the core sentence does not itself make the undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Rochester asks whether Jane will bear his infirmities and interprets doing so as sacrifice; Jane rejects that framing before making the highlighted avowal.\",\"supports\":\"The utterance functions as deliberate reassurance about love amid changed dependency, not merely as emotional intensity or spontaneous discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as an affirmative present-tense explicit love expression in Chapter XXXVII of Charlotte Brontë’s Jane Eyre.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the identified occurrence and its canonical textual location.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core comparative avowal, while a low P score records its secondary role in reinforcing the surrounding marital commitment. E is unnecessary because deliberation, explanation, and reassurance adequately characterize the event without positing reflex-like verbal discharge; no residual core force requires O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
